<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erich Ludendorff</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erich Ludendorff was a German general who served as chief of staff to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> on the Eastern Front from 1914, sharing in the victory at Tannenberg. Their command, Ober Ost, ran the occupied Baltic and Belarusian lands as a military state. From 1916 the two directed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s whole war effort. After 1918 Ludendorff joined Hitler's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> putsch of 1923 and spread the myth that the army had been stabbed in the back.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff.jpg" alt="Erich Ludendorff" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This tag does not indicate the copyright status of the attached work. A normal copyright tag is still required. See Commons:Licensing. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erich_Ludendorff_1924_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)(2)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Erich Ludendorff was born on 9 April 1865 at Kruszewnia near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a>, in the Prussian Province of Posen, the third of six children of a family descended from Pomeranian merchants. He was commissioned as a junior officer in 1885 and entered the War Academy in 1893. The next year he was recommended for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>, and from 1904 to 1913 he directed the Mobilisation Section of the Great General Staff in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> under Alfred von Schlieffen, where he helped oversee development of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan">Schlieffen Plan</a>. In 1910 he married Margarethe Schmidt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the outbreak of war in 1914 Ludendorff was deputy chief of staff of the German Second Army. When the brigade commander was killed, he led the assault that took Liège, and he received the Pour le Mérite. He was then made chief of staff to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> on the Eastern Front, and nine days after their arrival the Eighth Army surrounded most of a Russian army at Tannenberg and took 92,000 prisoners. On 29 August 1916 Hindenburg became head of the Supreme Army Command and Ludendorff First Quartermaster General with joint responsibility. From then they directed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s whole strategy and war effort, and put army officers into the economy and the ministries.</p><p>Their command supported unrestricted submarine warfare, begun in February 1917, and helped Lenin and about thirty other exiles return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> by train. On the Western Front they introduced an elastic defence in depth and, with Russia out of the war, launched Operation Michael on 21 March 1918. It made large advances but lost as many men as it killed, and among the dead was Ludendorff's eldest stepson. By late 1918 a string of defeats culminated in the Hundred Days Offensive of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and Kaiser Wilhelm II forced Ludendorff to resign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war Ludendorff became a prominent nationalist leader and promoted the stab-in-the-back myth, which blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat on a treasonous conspiracy of Marxists, Freemasons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. He took part in the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a> of 1920 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>'s Beer Hall Putsch of 1923, and stood unsuccessfully in the 1925 presidential election. In his last years he studied military theory, and his best-known work, The Total War, argued that a nation's whole resources should remain ready for mobilisation, because peace was merely an interval between wars. He died of liver cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 20 December 1937 and was given, against his wishes, a state funeral organised and attended by Hitler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Erich Ludendorff” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the German offensives of 1915 drove Russian armies out of Courland, Lithuania and the Grodno-Białystok region, the territory was placed under the Supreme Commander in the East, Oberbefehlshaber Ost, Paul von Hindenburg, with <b>Erich Ludendorff</b> as chief of staff.</blockquote><a href="/state/ober-ost">Ober Ost</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kruszewnia, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.37,17.1&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Kruszewnia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Erich Ludendorff"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4332 5585h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4332 5585h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.37,17.1&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
